  "documents": {
    "Native.md": "# Native\n\n## Purpose\n\nNative is the selected native execution subsystem. It gives the Rust implementation a shared context while retaining the C service as a representative source anchor rather than creating a page for every analyzed language component.\n\n## Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  C --> Native\n  Rust --> Native\n```\n\nThe [Rust](Rust.md) page describes the concrete implementation selected for source-level reading; the parent retains the C anchor as evidence for the broader native boundary.\n\n## Responsibilities\n\nNative explains the native subsystem boundary and the relationship between its representative source anchors and the documented implementation page.\n",
    "Platform.md": "# Platform\n\n## Purpose\n\nPlatform is the top-level subsystem that connects the selected native and scripting paths. It keeps the overview at the architectural boundary and leaves source details to the two child pages.\n\n## Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  Native --> Platform\n  Scripting --> Platform\n```\n\nThe [Native](Native.md) and [Scripting](Scripting.md) pages explain the two architectural branches and link onward to their representative implementation pages.\n\n## Responsibilities\n\nPlatform provides the end-to-end navigation boundary and the relationship between the selected execution and scripting surfaces.\n",
    "Python.md": "# Python\n\n## Purpose\n\nThe Python module contains the PythonService implementation in python/service.py. It is the source-level scripting service surface for this fixture and gives callers a concrete type to work with. This page explains the analyzed component instead of treating the directory name as an architectural boundary.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe PythonService implementation in python/service.py is a leaf with no recorded dependency edges. Its lifecycle is local: a caller reaches the service type, uses its fields or methods, and remains within this source file.\n\n## Responsibilities\n\nPythonService owns the scripting service representation. The [Scripting module](Scripting.md) supplies the parent architectural context.\n",
    "Rust.md": "# Rust\n\n## Purpose\n\nThe Rust module contains the RustService implementation in rust/service.rs. It is the source-level native service surface for this fixture and gives callers a concrete type to work with. This page explains the analyzed component instead of treating the directory name as an architectural boundary.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe RustService implementation in rust/service.rs is a leaf with no recorded dependency edges. Its lifecycle is local: a caller reaches the service type, uses its fields or methods, and remains within this source file.\n\n## Responsibilities\n\nRustService owns the native service representation. The [Native module](Native.md) supplies the parent architectural context.\n",
    "Scripting.md": "# Scripting\n\n## Purpose\n\nScripting is the selected scripting-facing subsystem. It keeps Python and TypeScript as representative anchors for the cross-language service surface while using one documented child to explain the source behavior.\n\n## Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  Python --> Scripting\n  TypeScript --> Scripting\n```\n\nThe [Python](Python.md) page provides the concrete source-grounded explanation; the TypeScript anchor remains evidence for the wider subsystem boundary.\n\n## Responsibilities\n\nScripting explains how the selected language-facing anchors form one reader-facing subsystem without turning every implementation variant into a page.\n",
    "overview.md": "# Replay Wiki\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis fixture repository demonstrates a cross-language service platform. The wiki organizes the analyzed implementations by architectural family so a reader can move from the end-to-end repository shape to a language-specific source page without mistaking directories for behavior.\n\n## Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  Client[Service client] --> Platform\n  Platform --> Native\n  Native --> Rust\n  Platform --> JVM\n  JVM --> Java\n  Platform --> Scripting\n  Scripting --> Python\n```\n\nThe [Platform](Platform.md) page is the starting point for the top-level architecture. It connects the family overviews to representative language implementations, which in turn link to the source-grounded leaf pages.\n\n## Responsibilities\n\nThis repository overview explains the request path through the platform, the subsystem relationships, and the boundary between architecture pages and implementation pages.\n"
  },
